Date:     Fri, 7 Jan 94 7:39:21 EST
From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject: What is a Singularity? (was: Technology/Politics)

Carl Feynman defines the singularity for our purposes as

"when the rate
of change becomes so large that we are unable to understand what happens
next"

By this definition, I think it happened at t=-infinity.
Certainly no later than the widespread application of the
printing press.

I think the concept is bogus.  I find no evidence that the accelerating
progress of science and technology, with new R&D building on the latest
enabling technology, is anything other than the exponential it is
usually tagged.  Exponentials don't have singularities.  Tangent functions,
for instance, do.  I can see how application of technology to the
development of technology can produce an exponential.  I don't see
how it can produce a tangent.

I think it is more operationally useful to talk in terms of relatively
near-term developments of specific technologies to the point where
specific important applications can be addressed economically.

And so my predictions... (drum roll, please)

Development of HARDWARE which could hold an uploaded mind and process
at rates comparable to the original wetware, or which could (if we
knew how to program it) hold an artificial intelligence roughly
comparable in capabilities to a human:

no earlier than 2010 (alien assistance excepted);

no later than 2140 (total collapse of civilization excepted);

best guess around 2065.

Looking back at my prediction, I recognize that my mind was
running concurrently on 2 tracks: I first denied the usefulness
of the original question and posed a replacement; then I tried
to answer an unasked question along the lines of the original.

I base this prediction on historic trends in computer memory
capacity and processing power, extrapolated to my estimate
of brainpower.

total number of neurons: [10^9..10^12]
synapses/neuron: [10^2..10^5]
firing rate: <0..10^4] /s
synaptic operation: multiply/accumulate
synaptic weight dynamic range: 10 bits

A gross estimate to be sure... and no idea how to program it.
