Date:     Fri, 7 Jan 94 8:40:50 EST
From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject:  FUND: game rules

It has been pointed out here that acceptance of government money
can be examined in a game-theoretic fashion.  I submit that any
behavior can be studied the same way, and that we should do so
more often in our discussions here.  We claim to be believers
in the free market.  Yet our individual choices are made in
the context of an unfree market at present.  Those choices
will be the basis of any market in which we participate.

It has been convincingly (to me) shown that the 'right' answer
(from a game-theoretic perspective) on acceptance of government
money is unstable.  that is, if everybody were rejecting it,
then each person would be 'right' to continue to reject it;
but if many are accepting it (indeed perhaps even one), then
it is 'right' to accept it.

This looks like a demonstration that our philosophy is not
robust!  If we believe that selfish individual actions
collectively produce a better result than socialist actions,
what about this counter-example? Admittedly, we are not
in a free market at present.  But how will we get there from
here if following our principle of selfishness doesn't do it?

As long as the individual benefits from the choice of taking
what the status quo can give, how can the status quo be
expected to evolve into something different?

I think we need to figure out how to change the game rules.
Because it appears that as long as the current rules pertain,
things will not evolve in the direction we would like.

How can we change the nature of the game so that the choice
which directly and immediately benefits the individual
ALSO drives the system in the direction we want?
