"This is one more reason I'm generally against "Big Science" and have
called for the death of the Supercollider (score one), the death of
the Space Station (a pretty good chance, as it's shrinking to the
point of meaninglessness), and the deaths of the Data Highway, the
Human Genome Project, and several other efforts."

--Tim May

As someone who is right now working at the Space Station Prime Contractor
office, I feel I have to update Mr. May's comment above.  The
"International Space Station 'Alpha' " (as it is called this week)
is not so much shrinking as it is starting over, with heritage of
the old 'Freedom' program.

The 'starting over' is addressing some, but not all, of the
fundamental problems the previous program had (such as sole
reliance on the Space Shuttle for delivery, and the multiple
prime contractors/multiple NASA centers management nightmare).

The program has been 'one-worlded', in the sense that the Russians
have been invited to join the program, and the configuration now
includes a union of what used to be Mir II and Freedom (slightly
modified), so basically the world's major space powers are now
all involved (except maybe China).

The new configuration actually has more volume, crew, and power
than the old Freedom had (from the addition of Russian elements).

There are still a load of technical issues and management issues
(for example, the Russian launch site, Baikonur, is in a different
country - Kazakhstan), but it is not the same program that it
was before, where Congress would trim it a little each year.

Speaking for myself - I have data points extending back to 1959,
when NASA first put together a long range plan, right up to today -
which all show a permanently occupied space station 8 years in the
future +- 1 year.  Given a 35 year trend, it would be imprudent
to assume that the Alpha station will reach permanent occupation
in 2002.

If you guys want more info on what is going on here, I'll be happy
to supply it.

Dani Eder


