 Working at the Institut Pasteur in Paris in the early 1960's, the
French biologists Francois Jacob and Jacques Monad had discovered
that a small fraction of the thousands of genes arrayed along the
DNA molecule can function as tiny switches. Turn one of these
switches on-by exposing the cell to a certain hormone, for example-
and the newly activated gene will send out a chemical signal to its
fellow genes. This signal will then travel up and down the length
of the DNA molecule and trip other genetic switches, flipping some
of them on and some of them off. These genes in turn, start sending
out chemical signals of there own (or stop sending them out). And
as a result, still more genetic switches will be tripped in a
mounting cascade, until the cell's collection of genes settles down
into a new and stable pattern.
 
 For biologists the implications of this discovery were enormous (
so much so that Jacob and Monad later shared the Nobel prize for
it). It meant that the DNA residing in a cell's nucleus was not
just a blueprint for the cell-a catalog of how to make this protein
or that protein. DNA was actually the foreman in charge of
construction. In effect, DNA was a kind of molecular-scale computer
that directed how the cell was to build itself and repair itself
and interact with the outside world. Furthermore, Jacob and Monad's
discovery solved the long standing mystery of how one fertilized
egg cell could divide and differentiate itself into muscle cells,
brain cells, liver cells, and all the other types of cells that
make up a newborn baby. Each different type of cell corresponded to
a different pattern of activated genes.


 From "Complexity" by M.Mitchell Waldrop ISBN 0-671-87234-6

Since I read this a few days ago it has had a profound affect on my
thinking. Like the chemical signals of DNA, every time I read this
new ideas cascade around my brain.

 It is clear that DNA is in fact a nanocomputer. It remains to be
seen if we shall one day possess this technology.


