I think where Mr. Newstrom and I differ is in what I call linear
thinking vs. exponential thinking.  Since he used space as an example,
I will respond in kind.  NASA planners like to lay things out in a
nicely spaced linear progression.  For example, the last Lunar/Mars
studies had the Space Station in 2000, a Lunar outpost in 2010, and
a manned Mars mission in 2020.  Partly the linear progression is
due to perceived funding available - which is about constant in
real dollars for NASA.

In reality, technological progress tends to go exponentially.  Projections
of military aircraft speeds going all the way back to 1920 show a trend
of 6% faster per year capability.  The trend is right on schedule
with the rumored Aurora spyplane in supposed use now, and either a
DC-X like SSTO or a airbreathing launcher being available around
2000.  Given a decent transport system to orbit, the Space Station,
Lunar base, and mars mission can then happen in rapid succession.
This is because a lunar base and a mars mission spacecraft can be
built with mostly the same hardware that you needed to build the
Space Station.

Things like driving a car automatically turn out to need fairly
massive computer power.  A few years ago it took a delivery
van full of computers to drive the van at 3 mph down a well marked
and lighted road (this was a DOD funded research project).  Assume
that the van full of equipment cost $1 million.  Then we can project
that in 20 years it will cost about $1000, and the average car buyer
might pay for it as an option.

We do, in fact have automated transportation - on commercial
aircraft.  A modern jet is capable of flying on autopilot from
takeoff to landing - assuming there is no unplanned rescheduling
due to other air traffic.  The autopilot system costs - no surprise -
on the order of a million $ - but since the airplane costs many
times this it can be economically justified.

When a product, such as car phones, reaches a level of cost vs
usefulness, all of a sudden everybody starts buying them.  The fact
that a bad futurist may have projected something to occur in
1980, and it really occurs in 2000, is not a comment on the pace
of technology - it is a comment on the skill of the futurist.

Dani Eder


